  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$",
